  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nA large mid-level ridge remains anchored over the southwestern CONUS\nand an area of low pressure is over southern Canada. There were a \nfew shortwaves noted between the ridge and the low to the north on \nwater vapor imagery early this morning. One was producing storms in \nNebraska and the other in far southeast Colorado. Some uncertainty \nremains on storm chances in the area today, but the most likely \nscenario is for increasing chances in central/north-central KS this \nafternoon, and then again across northeast KS tonight and early \nWednesday as a cold front associated with the system to the north \nmoves through the area. If storms make it into north-central KS this \nafternoon, the environment will be favorable for damaging wind gusts \nas instability increases and with DCAPE around 1000 J/kg. Shear will \nbe on the weaker side. Forcing does not look all that strong so have \nkept POPs low (20-30%) for now. Storms that develop tonight are less \nlikely to be severe with lower amounts of instability, more CIN and \ncontinued weak shear.\n\nA surface ridge builds in from the northwest behind the cold front \non Wednesday, and the pattern looks to remain dry through Friday. \nTemperatures and dew points will be just a touch lower through the \nend of the week. The pattern then shifts this weekend as the mid-\nlevel ridge flattens and moves toward the southeastern portion of the\nCONUS. The surface ridge will also move east, which will shift winds\nto the south. Temperatures respond by warming back into the 90s for \nboth Saturday and Sunday. Sunday will be the warmest day with strong \nsoutherly low level flow ahead of a surface trough in central KS. \nUnless widespread cloud cover or precipitation develops near the \ntrough axis, temperatures could warm into the mid and upper 90s with \nheat index values approaching 105 degrees. The hot weather will\ncontinue into early next week with at least slight chances for\nprecipitation returning to the forecast.",
